Therefore, the final result is $x \in (-\frac{1}{2}, \frac{7}{8}).$  $\boxed{(-\frac{1}{2}, \frac{7}{8})}.$

To find all $x$ such that $\lfloor \lfloor 2x \rfloor - 1/2 \rfloor = \lfloor x + 2 \rfloor,$ write $2x$ as $m + f,$ where $m \in \mathbb{Z},$ and $0 \le f < 1.$

We see that $\lfloor 2x \rfloor = m + 0.$  The point where $f = 1/2$ happens when $x \in \left( -\frac{1}{4}, \frac{7}{8} \right).$

On the interval $\left[ -\frac{1}{4}, \frac{7}{8} \right),$ $2x \in \left[ \frac{1}{2}, \frac{7}{4} \right).$  On this interval, $\lfloor 2x \rfloor$ is $1$ for $x \in \left[ -\frac{1}{4}, \frac{1}{4} \right),$ $2x \in \left[ 1, \frac{7}{4} \right),$ so $\lfloor 2x \rfloor = 1,$ and $\lfloor \lfloor 2x \rfloor - 1/2 \rfloor = 1,$ and of course $m = 1$ on this interval.

On the interval $\left( \frac{7}{8}, 3 \right],$ $2x \in (1, 2),$ so $2x \in \left[ 2, 3 \right),$ and $\lfloor 2x \rfloor = 2.$  In this interval, $\lfloor \lfloor 2x \rfloor - 1/2 \rfloor = 1.$ [asy]
